      "body": "On segregating Black children in public schools: \"To separate them from others of similar age and qualifications solely because of their race generates a feeling of inferiority as to their status in the community that may affect their hearts and minds in a way unlikely ever to be undone.\"\n\n\"We conclude that in the field of public education the doctrine of 'separate but equal' has no place. Separate educational facilities are inherently unequal.\"\n\nAbout the case: NAACP lawyers led by Thurgood Marshall argued Brown and four related cases on behalf of Black families in Kansas, South Carolina, Virginia, Delaware and the District of Columbia. The decision was unanimous. A second ruling in 1955 ordered desegregation \"with all deliberate speed,\" and many Southern officials responded with years of resistance.",
