    },
    {
      "attribution": "Civil Rights Act of 1964, Public Law 88-352, Title II, Section 201(a), signed July 2, 1964. Public domain (U.S. government work).",
      "body": "\"All persons shall be entitled to the full and equal enjoyment of the goods, services, facilities, privileges, advantages, and accommodations of any place of public accommodation, as defined in this section, without discrimination or segregation on the ground of race, color, religion, or national origin.\"\n\nAbout the source: Places of public accommodation included hotels, restaurants, theaters and gas stations. Other parts of the act barred discrimination in employment on the basis of race, color, religion, sex or national origin, and allowed the federal government to cut off funds to segregated programs. The act passed after the sit-in movement of 1960, the Freedom Rides of 1961, the Birmingham campaign and the March on Washington of 1963, and a Senate filibuster of more than fifty days.",
