      "title": "Civil Rights Act of 1964, Title II, Section 201(a)"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Lyndon B. Johnson, address to a joint session of Congress, March 15, 1965. Public domain (U.S. government work).",
      "body": "\"At times history and fate meet at a single time in a single place to shape a turning point in man's unending search for freedom. So it was at Lexington and Concord. So it was a century ago at Appomattox. So it was last week in Selma, Alabama.\"\n\n\"There is no Negro problem. There is no Southern problem. There is no Northern problem. There is only an American problem.\"\n\n\"And we shall overcome.\"\n\nContext: On March 7, 1965, state troopers and a sheriff's posse attacked peaceful voting rights marchers on the Edmund Pettus Bridge in Selma, Alabama; television carried the pictures nationwide. Johnson spoke eight days later. \"We shall overcome\" was the anthem of the civil rights movement. Congress passed the Voting Rights Act in August 1965. The Fifteenth Amendment (1870) already said that the right to vote \"shall not be denied or abridged by the United States or by any State on account of race, color, or previous condition of servitude,\" but Southern states had used poll taxes, literacy tests and violence to keep most Black citizens from voting.",
