    {
      "attribution": "Written for EducateOS: a composite letter drawing on the experiences Lowell mill workers described in their letters and in the Lowell Offering. Not a historical document; read it as a model source.",
      "body": "Lowell, May the 4th, 1845\n\nDear Sister,\n\nI take up my pen on the Sabbath, the only day the mill is still. You asked in your last whether I mean to come home for haying. I cannot say yet. I am now in the weaving room and tend three looms, and the overseer says that if I keep my cloth clean I may have a fourth by summer, which would raise my pay.\n\nI will not pretend it is easy. The bell rules us from before light until after dark. It wakes us at half past four, calls us to the mill, sends us to breakfast and back again, and does not let us go until seven in the evening. The noise of the looms is such that we talk with our hands, and the air is so full of lint that I cough at night. Two girls in our boardinghouse went home this spring with their health broken.\n\nBut I have money of my own for the first time. After my board is paid I put by near two dollars a week. I have sent three dollars in this letter toward Father's note at the bank, and I have bought a bonnet and a book of poems besides. There is a lecture hall, a library, and an improvement circle where the girls read their own compositions aloud. Mrs. Abbott, who keeps our house, is strict, and we must be in by ten and go to meeting on Sunday, but she is fair.\n\nSome of the girls are getting up a petition to the legislature for a ten-hour day. Several have signed. I have not signed yet, for some say the agents will put the names of those who sign on a list. Do not tell Mother I wrote of it.\n\nKiss the little ones for me. I shall write again when I have news of the fourth loom.\n\nYour affectionate sister,\nHannah",
