      "body": "Unemployment, percent of the civilian labor force:\n1929: 3\n1930: 9\n1931: 16\n1932: 24\n1933: 25\n1934: 22\n1935: 20\n1936: 17\n1937: 14\n1938: 19\n1939: 17\n1940: 15\n1941: 10\n1942: 5\n1943: 2\n1944: 1\n\nOther indicators:\nFrom 1929 to 1933, the economy's real output (real gross domestic product) fell by about one quarter.\nFrom 1930 to 1933, about 9,000 banks failed. Depositors in a failed bank usually lost their savings; there was no federal deposit insurance until 1933.\nThe Dow Jones Industrial Average, a measure of stock prices, peaked at about 381 in September 1929 and fell to about 41 in July 1932, a loss of almost 90 percent.\n\nKey events:\n1929: The stock market crashes in October.\n1932: Franklin D. Roosevelt is elected president.\n1933: Roosevelt takes office in March. The first New Deal creates federal relief programs, the Civilian Conservation Corps, and the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC).\n1935: The Works Progress Administration employs millions; Congress passes the Social Security Act and the National Labor Relations (Wagner) Act, which protects workers' right to join unions.\n1937: Believing recovery was under way, the government cuts spending while the Federal Reserve tightens credit. A sharp recession follows in 1937-1938.\n1941: The United States enters the Second World War in December. Federal spending on the war rises enormously from 1941 to 1945.",
